<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="a3e9b191">&lt;h3&gt;
    Engagement vs. Project
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The terms “engagement” and “project” are often used interchangeably by EY practitioners to refer to the work we do with
    our clients. However, these two terms are not synonymous and it is important to understand the distinction and
    relationship between projects and engagements; especially when we are discussing the methods, roles, and tools to
    support these two related disciplines and the scope of work that each represent.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    A project is a temporary work effort to achieve specific objectives within prescribed resource, time and quality
    constraints based on a set of well defined requirements. Projects achieve benefits for the client by providing a
    structured approach for managing the approach, scope, budget, time, quality, resources, communications, risk and issues
    for a specific, temporary, business endeavor. This is in contrast to day-to-day line management activities that guide
    ongoing business activities such as accounting and finance.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Clients may have many projects in progress, some of which may be grouped and managed as business programs. Programs are
    a management approach for dealing with very large project efforts. A program consists of a set of related projects that
    serve a common business purpose, but are divided into smaller manageable projects for control purposes and competency
    specialization. All of the projects in the program need to be completed for the program to be completed.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Our definition of project management is aligned with broadly accepted industry definitions. Engagement management,
    however, is a specific management discipline for professional services firms such as EY.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Engagement management is concerned with the commercial aspects of performing the work we do for clients. An engagement
    is a business commitment between EY and a client to deliver a specific scope of services within a specific timeframe,
    for agreed fees. Engagements are designed to address specific client issues and needs, service quality and value
    expectations. They are also designed to meet EY’s business needs in terms of profitability and growth.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Both projects and engagements require a specific scope to define them precisely. The scope of an EY engagement, might
    address one client project, some portion of a project, multiple projects within a client program, or an entire program.
    Under this definition, the projects and programs define the work that needs to be accomplished to achieve business
    objectives, and the engagement defines the specific scope of work that EY is contracted to perform.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
